Yes, the docs support this pattern. A component is a class that extends `ComponentResource`, and its child resources are created in the constructor. Pass `parent: this` to each child, including child components, so they nest under the big component in the resource graph.

The docs confirm nested components in two ways. First, `dependsOn: [myComponent]` covers the resources the component reaches, "including those inside nested components." Second, options set on a component apply to resources it creates, including ones added later. The docs don't include a full example of a component that composes a database and network component, so the snippet below follows the documented pattern. The docs' own `AcmeVpc` example is reused for the network part.

```typescript
class WebApp extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
    constructor(name: string, args: WebAppArgs, opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions) {
        super("acme:index:WebApp", name, {}, opts);

        // Child component for the network, parented to WebApp
        const network = new AcmeVpc(`${name}-network`, {
            cidrBlock: "10.0.0.0/16",
        }, { parent: this });

        // Your database component would follow the same pattern, with parent: this
        // and a name templated from `name`.

        this.registerOutputs({});
    }
}
```

Key points from the docs:
- Template each child's name with the component's `name` parameter (for example `${name}-network`). Hard-coded child names cause duplicate-URN errors when the component is used more than once.
- Setting `protect`, `providers`, or similar options on the outer component (for example `new WebApp("prod", args, { protect: true })`) applies them to every child, including nested ones. Not every option is inherited, so check the options reference.
- Call `registerOutputs` at the end of the constructor, even with an empty object, so the component is marked as fully constructed.

I don't have your code, so I can't tell whether your existing database and network components are TypeScript classes. The docs describe the same pattern for TypeScript, Python, Go, C#, and Java, but I haven't verified the exact syntax for each. If your team wants the composed component used from another language, the docs say it can be packaged and consumed from any Pulumi language.

To nest a component inside another component, create the child component inside the parent's constructor and pass the parent as the `parent` resource option (`{ parent: this }`). The docs describe this as the normal way to build hierarchies of components.

Why this works: the parent option sets an explicit parent/child relationship. The docs state that resources inside a component "should be explicitly parented to the component itself," and that "You can also create multiple levels of nesting within a component to improve the visual display in the CLI."

The IDP pattern doc shows a higher-level component that uses lower-level components (TypeScript):

```typescript
export class WebApplication extends ComponentResource {
  constructor(name: string, args: WebApplicationArgs, opts?: ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("acme:patterns:WebApplication", name, {}, opts);

    // Use network component
    const network = new Network(`${name}-network`, {
      cidrBlock: "10.0.0.0/16",
    }, { parent: this });

    // Use database component
    const database = new Database(`${name}-db`, {
      instanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
      storage: 20,
      username: args.dbUsername,
      password: args.dbPassword,
      dbName: args.dbName,
    }, { parent: this });
  }
}
```

The nested component's own constructor takes `opts?: ComponentResourceOptions` and passes it to `super(...)`, as shown in the Database and Network classes in the same doc. Other languages use the same idea. The parent option examples show Python as `ResourceOptions(parent=parent)`, C# as `new CustomResourceOptions { Parent = parent }`, and Go as `pulumi.Parent(parent)`.

What a nested child inherits from its parent: the parent doc lists `provider`, `aliases`, `protect`, `retainOnDelete`, `transforms`, `transformations`, and `deletedWith`. Only these are inherited, and a child can set an option explicitly to override it. For example, a child can set `retainOnDelete: false` to opt out.

Also, child names should be templated from the component's `name` argument (for example `${name}-network`). The docs warn that hard-coded child names cause duplicate-URN errors when the component is instantiated more than once.

No code from your project was provided, so I could not extend your existing code. If you share your component classes and language, I can show how to apply this to them. The docs' examples are in TypeScript, with equivalents for other languages in the parent option page.

The retrieved docs show the Database component exposing `connectionString` and `securityGroupId` as class properties (`public readonly connectionString: Output<string>;` and `public readonly securityGroupId: Output<string>;`). The WebApplication example does not finish the wiring. Inside it, the `ecs.Service` body only has the comment `// Use network.vpcId and database.connectionString`, and WebApplication has no output properties or `registerOutputs` call. So the docs do not show a complete end-to-end example of getting these values out of WebApplication.

The documented pattern is: store the values in class properties, then call `registerOutputs`. The build-a-component guide says outputs should be class properties and that `registerOutputs` saves them to state so they appear in the CLI and Console. Applying that pattern to your WebApplication (TypeScript, as in the docs) looks like this:

```typescript
export class WebApplication extends ComponentResource {
  public readonly connectionString: Output<string>;
  public readonly securityGroupId: Output<string>;

  constructor(name: string, args: WebApplicationArgs, opts?: ComponentResourceOptions) {
    super("acme:patterns:WebApplication", name, {}, opts);

    const database = new Database(`${name}-db`, {
      instanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
      storage: 20,
      username: args.dbUsername,
      password: args.dbPassword,
      dbName: args.dbName,
    }, { parent: this });

    const app = new aws.ecs.Service(`${name}-app`, {
      // use database.connectionString and database.securityGroupId here
    }, { parent: this });

    this.connectionString = database.connectionString;
    this.securityGroupId = database.securityGroupId;

    this.registerOutputs({
      connectionString: database.connectionString,
      securityGroupId: database.securityGroupId,
    });
  }
}
```

The consumer then reads the values from the instance:

```typescript
const myApp = new WebApplication("my-web-app", {
  dbUsername: "admin",
  dbPassword: dbPassword,
  dbName: "myapp",
});

export const connectionString = myApp.connectionString;
export const securityGroupId = myApp.securityGroupId;
```

The `this.connectionString = ...` and `registerOutputs` lines follow the documented pattern, but the docs do not show them in WebApplication itself. The `export const` lines are not covered by the retrieved documents, so check the stack-outputs docs for that part. The `ecs.Service` configuration is left as a comment in the docs, so you will need to pass the values into your app's configuration yourself.

Yes, that is expected behavior for the deterministic converter. The docs say `pulumi convert` translates HCL construct by construct and emits a TODO wherever it meets something it can't translate, so its output mirrors the shape of the HCL rather than the idioms of the target language. It is a fast, repeatable first pass, not a finished program.

The docs say the converter handles what it recognizes and leaves a TODO for the rest, including heavy `for_each` and `dynamic` blocks and module indirection. The TypeScript example in the docs looks like normal code because it covers a simple configuration with no unsupported constructs. Your output will look different if your source uses constructs the converter can't translate.

