You can block oversized databases with a custom policy pack that checks each RDS instance's `instanceClass`, then attach it to a dev-only policy group with its own allowed list. The docs don't include a ready-made database-size policy, so this adapts the documented RDS pattern and configuration approach. The examples are AWS RDS; you didn't say which cloud or language you use, so I've used TypeScript and AWS RDS.

**1. Write the policy pack**

The policy uses `validateResourceOfType(aws.rds.Instance, ...)`, the same pattern the docs use for RDS, and reads its allowed list from configuration with `configSchema` and `args.getConfig`:

```typescript
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import { PolicyPack, validateResourceOfType } from "@pulumi/policy";

new PolicyPack("db-size-policies", {
    policies: [{
        name: "rds-allowed-instance-class",
        description: "Restricts RDS instance classes to the list configured for each policy group.",
        enforcementLevel: "mandatory",
        configSchema: {
            properties: {
                allowedInstanceClasses: { type: "array", items: { type: "string" } },
            },
            required: ["allowedInstanceClasses"],
        },
        validateResource: validateResourceOfType(aws.rds.Instance, (instance, args, reportViolation) => {
            const config = args.getConfig<{ allowedInstanceClasses: string[] }>();
            if (!config.allowedInstanceClasses.includes(instance.instanceClass)) {
                reportViolation(`RDS instance class '${instance.instanceClass}' is not allowed here. Allowed: ${config.allowedInstanceClasses.join(", ")}.`);
            }
        }),
    }],
});
```

Publish it with `pulumi policy publish <org-name>`.

**2. Set allowed sizes per environment**

The docs show that a config file can set the pack-wide enforcement level with `"all"` and per-policy values by policy name. Create `dev-sizes.json`:

```json
{
    "all": "mandatory",
    "rds-allowed-instance-class": {
        "allowedInstanceClasses": ["db.t3.micro", "db.t3.small"]
    }
}
```

Create a separate file for prod with a larger list. Then enable the pack on the matching group:

```sh
pulumi policy enable <org>/db-size-policies <version> --config dev-sizes.json --policy-group dev-policy-group
```

**3. Put each environment's stacks in its own group**

The policy groups doc recommends one group per environment tier, because enforcement levels are set per pack and per policy. Add your dev stacks to the dev group and prod stacks to the prod group. Stack membership can be managed in the console, through the REST API, or with the `PolicyGroupStackAttachment` resource in the Pulumi Cloud provider.

**Things to avoid**

Do not add this pack to `default-policy-group`. It contains every stack in the organization, and a `mandatory` policy there starts blocking deployments on the next `pulumi preview` or `pulumi up` with no gradual rollout. Also, preventative policies only see resources Pulumi manages, so a database created by hand in the console won't be checked. An audit group can cover those.

To test before enforcing, you can run `pulumi preview --policy-pack <path> --policy-pack-config dev-sizes.json` locally. The docs show this flag for local runs.

