You can enforce this with a Pulumi Policy Pack (CrossGuard) that has a mandatory resource validation policy, attached to a preventative policy group so it runs during `pulumi preview` and `pulumi up` on your stacks. The docs also show a cost-control pattern that uses tags on database resources, which the example below follows.

**1. Write the policy (TypeScript, from the cost-control pattern, with a standard import)**

```typescript
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";
import { PolicyPack, validateResourceOfType } from "@pulumi/policy";

const allowedSizesByEnvironment: Record<string, string[]> = {
    dev: ["small"],
    staging: ["small", "medium"],
    prod: ["small", "medium", "large"],
};

new PolicyPack("cost-control-policies", {
    policies: [{
        name: "database-cost-control",
        description: "Enforce cost controls on database instances",
        enforcementLevel: "mandatory",
        validateResource: validateResourceOfType(aws.rds.Instance, (instance, args, reportViolation) => {
            const size = instance.tags?.["Size"];
            const environment = instance.tags?.["Environment"];
            if (size && environment) {
                const allowed = allowedSizesByEnvironment[environment];
                if (allowed && !allowed.includes(size)) {
                    reportViolation(`${environment} environment only allows sizes: ${allowed.join(", ")}`);
                }
            }
        }),
    }],
});
```

Note that this check only runs when a database has both `Size` and `Environment` tags. A database without those tags is not checked, so the pattern relies on components that set them. The docs' cost pattern sets them in a `CostControlledDatabase` component.

**2. Test locally** against a program with `pulumi preview --policy-pack <path-to-policy-pack-directory>`. A violation shows up as a `[mandatory]` entry and blocks the preview.

**3. Publish** the pack with `pulumi policy publish <org-name>`. Each version can only be published once, so bump the version for every change.

**4. Attach it to stacks.** In the console, go to **Governance** > **Policy configuration** > **Policy Groups** > **Create preventative policy group**, choose the stacks, select the pack, and set its enforcement level. From the CLI, use `pulumi policy enable <org>/<pack-name> <version> --config config.json --policy-group <group-name>`.

**Rollout caution:** `default-policy-group` applies to every stack in the organization automatically. Adding a pack there with a mandatory policy starts blocking deployments immediately, with no gradual rollout. The docs recommend this order: first an audit policy group to see the existing backlog, then a preventative group with everything advisory, then promote specific policies to mandatory. Mandatory enforcement is listed under Pro, while Essentials reports organization-managed policies in advisory mode.

I did not check whether a pre-built policy pack covers database cost, so this custom pack is the approach the docs describe for your case.

